// ============================================================
// Process: reading an operator chain as a PROMPT TRANSFORMATION POLICY
// Worked instance: "Research about torsion field."
//                  Apply Axis ∘ Ana ∘ Pro ∘ Para ∘ Kata ∘ Latch.
//
// Tags used in comments:
//   [REASONING]  -> judgment call / creative choice, not computed
//   [COMPUTED]   -> deterministic math or a literal field read
//   [VERIFIED]   -> ran real code to check a claim, not trusted by eye
//
// Real imports. Everything tagged [COMPUTED] reads one of these rather than
// restating a constant in a comment, so the pseudocode cannot drift from the
// kernel the way a copied "0.75" silently would.
//
//   import {
//     operatorMeaning, operatorSymbol, operatorClass, lambdaIntrinsic,
//   } from "../kernel/formalism.js";
//   import { operatorEffect } from "../kernel/phasePortrait.js";
//   import { analyzeSequence } from "../kernel/dissipation.js";
//   import { lookupOperator, formatAuthoredLambda } from "../vocab/operators.js";
//   import { checkForbiddenSequence, CONSTRAINT } from "../vocab/grammar.js";
//   import { executionMode, budgetForBand } from "../vocab/execution-classes.js";
//   import { lambdaBandFor } from "../ir/compile.js";
//
// The vocab layer matters more here than the kernel does. src/vocab/
// execution-classes.ts ALREADY answers the two questions this process most
// wants to ask — "what does an operator of this class let the executor do"
// (class -> ExecutionMode) and "how much effort does an operator of this λ
// buy" (λ band -> ExecutionBudget). An earlier draft of this file authored
// both mappings by hand. They did not need authoring; they needed importing.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// The one rule this whole file exists to enforce
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// A chain is NOT a schedule of six sections. Rendering "∘" as an ordered list
// of headings — "Step 1 (Axis): do the framing. Step 2 (Ana): do the
// analysis." — is the same class of error as inventing a measurement: it
// invents STRUCTURE the notation does not carry. `∘` is composition. What
// composes is a set of PROPERTIES the finished prompt must exhibit, all of
// them at once, in every paragraph. Axis ∘ Ana does not mean "frame, then
// analyse"; it means "analytical, within a fixed frame".
//
// The discipline that replaces it, mirroring no-invented-measurement.ts:
// every clause in the output prompt must be traceable to a NAMED FIELD of a
// named operator, or to a named field of the ExecutionMode / ExecutionBudget
// that operator resolves to. A clause a reviewer cannot trace to one of those
// is a clause the chain did not license, however good it sounds.
// ============================================================


function fieldsToProperty(op):

    // Step 1 — read all NINE formalism fields, then the two vocab records they
    // resolve to. None is decorative; each binds to a different part of the
    // emitted constraint.
    r = formalism.operators[op]
    require r has { index, name, symbol, meaning, class,
                    lambda_intrinsic, effect_vector, effect,
                    idempotent, idempotence_rule? }

    // 1a — `meaning` is the property's ADJECTIVE, not its verb.
    // [REASONING] this is the whole reframe and it is a reading, not a
    // computation. "Break apart, raise conceptual level" read as an
    // instruction yields a step ("break the topic apart"). Read as a
    // characteristic it yields an adjective ("analytical") that every
    // paragraph must satisfy. The formalism does not say which reading is
    // correct. `∘` does: composition of steps would be `;`.
    property.adjective = adjectiveOf(operatorMeaning(op))

    // 1b — `class` resolves to a real ExecutionMode. Not authored here.
    mode = executionMode(op)          // real import, vocab/execution-classes.js
    property.move      = mode.cognitiveMove
    property.mayCommit = "write" in mode.capabilities
    property.exitTest  = mode.requiredArtifact
    // [VERIFIED] ran executionMode over the chain against dist/:
    //   Axis  D-Structural   "frame, integrate, or freeze structure"
    //                        caps read,write   -> "a named frame, hinge, or committed contract"
    //   Ana   B-Disruptive   "diverge, attack, rupture the current framing"
    //                        caps read         -> "at least two alternatives, or one counterexample"
    //   Pro   A-Constructive "converge, concretize, commit"
    //                        caps read,write   -> "a concrete edit or a stated decision"
    //   Para  B-Disruptive   (same as Ana)
    //   Kata  A-Constructive (same as Pro)
    //   Latch A-Constructive (same as Pro)
    //
    // `capabilities` is the sharpest thing in this whole process and it is
    // authored policy, not formalism. B-Disruptive is READ-ONLY by
    // construction — the table's own docstring: "a step whose job is to attack
    // the current answer must not be able to commit one". So in this chain the
    // analytical and adversarial properties may NEVER be the properties that
    // settle a question. Only Axis, Pro, Kata, Latch may commit. That single
    // fact does more to shape the output prompt than any adjective does.
    //
    // `requiredArtifact` is the property's EXIT TEST, already written. A
    // prompt clause that carries an adjective but no artifact requirement has
    // dropped the checkable half of the operator.

    // 1c — `effect` is the LICENSE: the one thing this property permits that
    // the others do not.
    // [COMPUTED] literal field read; no accessor exists (Finding 3).
    property.license = r.effect
    // Ana's "increases entropy" is a permission slip — under Ana the claim set
    // is ALLOWED to get messier, and a prompt that forbids that has dropped
    // the operator while still naming it.

    // 1d — `idempotent` + `idempotence_rule` decide the property's LIFETIME.
    // This is the field that most distinguishes a policy from a step list, and
    // the one a step-list rendering wastes entirely.
    // [REASONING] the mapping below is authored; the formalism states the
    // algebra, not its prose consequence. Nothing in src/ reads these fields.
    switch r.idempotent:
        case true:   property.lifetime = STANDING_INVARIANT
                     // X² = X. Re-applying changes nothing, so the property
                     // can hold over the whole prompt without accumulating.
                     // Safe to phrase as "at all times". (Kata, Latch)
        case "semi": property.lifetime = DECAYING_LICENSE
                     // rule gives the coefficient: Pro² = 0.5·Pro. A second
                     // application returns half. Phrase as "once, and once
                     // more only if the first pass left something open".
        case false:  property.lifetime = ONE_SHOT_LICENSE
                     // algebra_relations.idempotence.never lists Ana, Para,
                     // Non, Retro. Every re-application is a NEW expansion,
                     // not a sharpening. Phrase as an explicit budget:
                     // "one pass, and do not re-open". (Axis, Ana, Para)

    // 1e — `effect_vector` is the property's DIRECTION on the two axes the
    // kernel actually tracks. [COMPUTED] real import.
    [dD, dC] = operatorEffect(op)
    //   dD -> breadth: how much material the prompt admits
    //   dC -> tension: how much unresolved disagreement it tolerates
    // [REASONING] naming the axes "breadth" and "tension" for prompt text is a
    // reading. In the kernel D is dissipation and C is contradiction
    // curvature, about a reasoning process, not about a document.

    // 1f — `lambda_intrinsic` buys a real BUDGET. Also not authored here.
    band   = lambdaBandFor(lambdaIntrinsic(op))     // real import, ir/compile.js
    budget = budgetForBand(band)                    // real import, vocab/
    property.fanOut    = budget.fanOut
    property.verify    = budget.verify
    property.modelTier = budget.modelTier
    // [VERIFIED] thresholds are compile.ts:21-25 (<0.4 low, <=0.7 mid, else
    // high). Over the chain:
    //   Ana   0.75 high -> fanOut 5, verify adversarial, tier strongest
    //   Para  0.65 mid  -> fanOut 3, verify single,      tier standard
    //   Pro   0.50 mid  -> fanOut 3, verify single,      tier standard
    //   Kata  0.35 low  -> fanOut 1, verify optional,    tier cheap
    //   Axis  0.31 low  -> fanOut 1, verify optional,    tier cheap
    //   Latch 0.29 low  -> fanOut 1, verify optional,    tier cheap
    //
    // [REASONING] read as prose: λ is a DISSIPATION rate, so high λ means the
    // property degrades fastest, not that it matters most — and the budget
    // table agrees, spending its effort exactly where λ is high. The prose
    // consequence is that Ana's clause must be the most explicitly reinforced
    // in the emitted prompt (five candidate framings, adversarially checked)
    // while Latch's needs to be stated once. The BUDGET numbers are real; the
    // decision to spend them on sentences rather than on agent turns is the
    // reading.

    // 1g — `symbol` + `index` + `name` are the audit handle.
    // [COMPUTED] carried so every emitted clause can be tagged back to its
    // source operator, which is what makes the rule at the top checkable.
    property.tag = operatorSymbol(op) + " " + r.name + " #" + r.index

    return property


function composePolicy(chain):

    // Step 2 — legality first, through the vocab layer rather than the kernel,
    // because vocab/grammar.js attaches a NAMED constraint id to a rejection
    // and the kernel returns a bare boolean.
    result = checkForbiddenSequence(chain)          // real import, vocab/grammar.js
    if not result.accepted:
        abort with result.constraint, result.reason
    // [VERIFIED] ran it on [Axis, Ana, Pro, Para, Kata, Latch] against dist/:
    // { accepted: true }. All five named constraints pass — META_MAX_TWO,
    // META_THEN_NON, NON_THEN_PARA, END_ON_ANA, VALE_STABILIZER — three of them
    // vacuously (no Meta, Non, or Vale in the chain). Length 6 is inside
    // inverse_solver.termination.max_path_length (14).

    properties = [fieldsToProperty(op) for op in chain]

    // Step 3 — the net vector. This is what makes the composition a POLICY:
    // the properties are simultaneous, so their directions add.
    net = sum(operatorEffect(op) for op in chain)   // real import
    // [VERIFIED] net = ΔD -0.2100, ΔC -0.0900, summed by the real
    // operatorEffect table. Both negative ⇒ the composed policy is
    // NET-CONTRACTIVE on both axes: the finished prompt must admit less
    // material and tolerate less unresolved tension than it started with.
    // That is what forbids the disruptive properties (Ana, Para) from being
    // rendered as an open-ended "explore freely".
    //
    // [REASONING] the sums are of AUTHORED constants.
    // DEFAULT_OPERATOR_EFFECTS' own docstring says the vectors are "generated
    // from operator class, not measured" and that no value "should be cited as
    // evidence about" an observed outcome. So -0.21 licenses a DIRECTION
    // (contract) and nothing finer. Do not report it as a magnitude.
    //
    // 3b — and it does not stand alone. The capability table in Step 1b
    // reaches the same conclusion through a completely independent authored
    // table: the two OPENING operators are the two that cannot commit. Two
    // tables written for different purposes agreeing on which properties may
    // close the document is the strongest evidence in this file. It is still
    // two authored tables, not a measurement.

    // Step 4 — conflict resolution. Ana says diverge, Kata says converge, and
    // as a step list that was a schedule. As a policy it is a contradiction
    // that needs a rule.
    //
    //   4a. On each axis, the SIGN OF THE SUM decides the standing direction.
    //       [COMPUTED] both negative here ⇒ convergence wins both.
    //   4b. The divergent properties survive as bounded licenses, not as
    //       directions — and Step 1b makes that mechanical rather than
    //       stylistic: they are read-only, so they can require alternatives
    //       and counterexamples but cannot be what the prompt ends on.
    //   4c. Terminal character is set by last(chain).
    //       [REASONING] justified by the formalism's own hard constraint
    //       "Ana at sequence end: FORBIDDEN (must end concrete)", carried in
    //       vocab/grammar.js as CONSTRAINT.END_ON_ANA. That constraint only
    //       makes sense if the final operator governs how the output
    //       terminates — the formalism is asserting exactly this rule, for
    //       exactly one operator. Generalising it is the reading. Here
    //       last = Latch: "locking stabilization", A-Constructive, may commit,
    //       idempotent ⇒ the prompt terminates evidence-stabilized and stays
    //       that way.

    // Step 5 — the seams. Adjacent pairs are where a composed policy tears,
    // and the kernel prices each one.
    costs = analyzeSequence(chain).pairwiseCosts    // real import
    // [VERIFIED] against dist/kernel/dissipation.js:
    //   Axis → Ana   λ 0.90   ← highest: the scoped/analytical seam
    //   Ana  → Pro   λ 0.65
    //   Pro  → Para  λ 0.80   ← second: the forward/adversarial seam
    //   Para → Kata  λ 0.50
    //   Kata → Latch λ 0.44   ← lowest: synthesis into lock is nearly free
    //   λ_eff 0.658, totalCost 3.29, halfLife 1.053
    //   lambdaBandFor(0.658) = "mid" (see Finding 5)
    //
    // [REASONING] the prose consequence: the two expensive seams are the two
    // places the prompt must explicitly BIND its properties together, because
    // they are the joints most likely to come apart —
    //   Axis→Ana : say what the analysis is analysis OF, in the frame's terms
    //   Pro→Para : say that the adversarial pass attacks the PROPAGATED
    //              claims, not a straw restatement of them.
    // Pricing a sentence with a cognitive dissipation rate is an ANALOGY.
    // Nothing has been measured about prompt text. It ranks the seams; it does
    // not quantify them.
    //
    // 5b. halfLife 1.053 steps under decay_law D(t+1) = D(t)·exp(-λ_eff).
    // [REASONING] read as prose: a property mentioned once is below half
    // strength by the next property. Hence every property is rendered as a
    // STANDING constraint restated in place, never as a heading that fires
    // once. Same analogy caveat — this is why the output has no numbered
    // sections, and it is the strongest structural claim in the file resting
    // on the weakest evidence.

    return { properties, net, costs, terminal: last(chain) }


function renderPrompt(intent, policy):

    // Step 6 — emit. One brief. No per-operator headings: a heading per
    // operator is the step-list error re-entering through layout.
    // [REASONING] house style is the six adjectives as a single standing
    // description, each carrying its exit test, its commit right, and its
    // lifetime inline.

    for p in policy.properties:
        emit clause(p.adjective, p.exitTest, p.mayCommit, p.lifetime, p.license)
        reinforce clause in proportion to p.fanOut / p.verify
        annotate clause with p.tag        // audit handle, strippable

    // For the worked instance the six properties resolve to:
    //
    //  ⊢ Axis  #17  scoped              may commit   one-shot   named frame
    //  ↑ Ana   #1   analytical          READ ONLY    one-shot   ≥2 alternatives or 1 counterexample
    //  ↷ Pro   #9   forward-looking     may commit   decaying   a stated decision
    //  ∥ Para  #4   adversarial         READ ONLY    one-shot   ≥2 alternatives or 1 counterexample
    //  ↓ Kata  #2   synthesized         may commit   standing   a stated decision
    //  ⊣ Latch #20  evidence-stabilized may commit   standing   a stated decision
    //
    // and Step 3's net-contractive result, corroborated by the two READ ONLY
    // rows, forbids any clause that would let the brief end open.

    return brief


function verifyPrompt(brief, policy, chain):

    // Step 7 — the traceability check, cheap, so there is no excuse.
    for clause in brief:
        assert clause traces to a named field of an operator in chain,
               or to a field of its ExecutionMode / ExecutionBudget
    // [REASONING] the assert is by eye — there is no parser for this. It is
    // still worth writing down, because "which field licensed this sentence?"
    // is answerable for every sentence in the worked output, and that is the
    // property this whole file is defending.

    // Step 8 — the coverage check.
    for op in chain:
        assert some clause carries operatorSymbol(op)          // real import
    // A chain member with no surviving clause was dropped, not composed.

    // Step 9 — the exit-test check. Stronger than Step 8 and mechanical.
    for op in chain:
        assert executionMode(op).requiredArtifact is demanded somewhere in brief
    // [VERIFIED] requiredArtifact is per CLASS, not per operator, so the
    // demands collapse hard: this chain's six operators span three classes
    // (D-Structural, B-Disruptive, A-Constructive) and therefore make only
    // THREE distinct demands. A brief that demands "at least two alternatives,
    // or one counterexample" exactly once has satisfied both Ana and Para with
    // a single clause, and one "a concrete edit or a stated decision" covers
    // Pro, Kata and Latch together — check whether that was intended before
    // accepting it. Step 8's per-symbol coverage check is what keeps the
    // individual operators visible; this check alone would not.

    // Step 10 — the negative check, which catches the failure mode this file
    // was written to prevent.
    assert brief has no section-per-operator
    assert brief does not order the reader through the chain
    // [REASONING] if the brief can be split into six blocks that each mention
    // exactly one operator, the policy reading was lost in rendering.

    return brief


// ============================================================
// What checking this against the real kernel and vocab layer found
// ============================================================
//
// Finding 1 — formalism.json's stated formula and the code disagree, and the
// code is what runs.
// [VERIFIED] dissipation_rules.formula reads
//     λ(i→j) = λ_j_intrinsic + c·min(0.4, |η_ij|)
// and dissipation.ts:24's own docstring restates it as
//     lambda_j + c*min(max, |commutator|)
// but dissipation.ts:26-30 computes
//     lambda_j + min(c*|commutator|, max)
// — the multiply and the min are swapped. For |η| = 1 and c = 0.15 the JSON
// formula gives +0.06 and the code gives +0.15. Swept all 400 pairs: the two
// readings differ on 328 of them, agreeing only where magnitude is 0.
// Consequence for this chain: λ_eff is 0.658 (code) versus 0.568 (JSON
// formula), and halfLife 1.05 versus 1.22. Every number in Step 5 is the
// CODE's, because the code is the thing that ships. Whichever is intended,
// formalism.json currently documents a formula the kernel does not implement.
//
// Finding 2 — max_interaction_magnitude is dead under the current skeleton.
// [VERIFIED] magnitudes in commutator_skeleton.json span [0.0, 1.0], so
// c·|η| ≤ 0.15 and the 0.4 clamp never binds — zero of 400 pairs reach it.
// Under the JSON formula's ordering the clamp would bind for every |η| > 0.4,
// i.e. 340 of 400 pairs. The constant is load-bearing in the documented
// formula and inert in the implemented one, which is corroborating evidence
// that Finding 1 is a genuine transposition rather than a deliberate change.
//
// Finding 3 — three of the nine operator fields have no accessor anywhere.
// [VERIFIED] formalism.ts exports operatorMeaning, operatorSymbol,
// operatorClass, lambdaIntrinsic, operatorEffectVector; vocab/operators.ts
// re-exports name, class, λ, symbol as AuthoredOperator. Nothing anywhere
// reads `effect`, `idempotent`, or `idempotence_rule`. Steps 1c and 1d — the
// license and the LIFETIME, the field carrying the entire policy-vs-steps
// distinction — are served by reading formalism.json directly. Anything built
// on this file should add those accessors to vocab/operators.ts first, beside
// the four it already adapts; a second hand-rolled read of formalism.json is
// exactly the drift that file's docstring says it exists to prevent.
//
// Finding 4 — algebra_relations is inert data, and I over-claimed on it.
// [VERIFIED] grepped src for absorption_laws, triple_relations,
// neutral_commutations, algebra_relations: zero readers. Nothing in the kernel
// applies "Para ∘ Kata = Ana", so an earlier claim that the Para→Kata segment
// is "formally reducible" describes a line of JSON no code consults. Under the
// policy reading it would not apply anyway: absorption rewrites a composition
// of steps, and these are simultaneous properties. Left standing as a question
// for whoever wires algebra_relations up — it is the only place the formalism
// says two named operators COLLAPSE, which a policy composer would need a
// story for.
//
// Finding 5 — per-operator band and whole-chain band disagree, and nothing
// says which one a composed policy should spend.
// [VERIFIED] lambdaBandFor(0.658) = "mid" for the chain as a whole (fanOut 3,
// verify single, tier standard), while Ana alone is "high" (fanOut 5, verify
// adversarial, tier strongest). compileIR (ir/compile.ts:29) bands the
// EFFECTIVE λ of a session's sequence, i.e. it spends the chain-level budget;
// Step 1f above spends the per-operator one. Averaging λ across a chain
// averages away exactly the operator that needed the adversarial pass. For a
// step list the distinction is minor. For a policy — where all properties hold
// at once and there is no "during Ana" to spend a bigger budget in — it is
// unresolved, and this file spends the per-operator budget without a stated
// justification beyond "it is the one that preserves the information".
//
// Finding 6 — no execution class is granted `network`, and this is a research
// chain.
// [VERIFIED] EXECUTION_MODES grants capabilities read (B-Disruptive,
// C-Reflexive) and read,write (A-Constructive, D-Structural). `shell` and
// `network` are granted to no class, deliberately — the table's docstring says
// "a prototype does not need them, and widening a capability should be a
// deliberate edit". So a chain executed through this engine cannot fetch a
// source. The torsion-field brief this file renders is a research brief whose
// exit tests (counterexamples, cited alternatives) cannot be satisfied by any
// agent the capability table would authorise. Either the brief is for a human
// or an out-of-engine model, or D-Structural/A-Constructive need `network` —
// and that is the deliberate edit the docstring is asking for.
//
// Finding 7 — the chain contains no corrective operator.
// [COMPUTED] classes present: D-Structural (Axis), B-Disruptive (Ana, Para),
// A-Constructive (Pro, Kata, Latch). Absent: Ortho ("Correction/Alignment",
// "aligns with truth", λ 0.30) and Telo (terminal absorber). The composed
// policy is contractive and stabilizing but has no property that checks the
// synthesized claims against ground truth — Latch locks whatever Kata placed,
// including whatever Para destabilized. For a topic where one referent of the
// name is a well-documented pseudoscience claim, that gap is the difference
// between "evidence-stabilized" and "confidently wrong".
// [VERIFIED] Axis ∘ Ana ∘ Pro ∘ Para ∘ Ortho ∘ Kata ∘ Latch passes
// checkForbiddenSequence, and moves λ_eff 0.658 -> 0.6233, totalCost
// 3.29 -> 3.74 (+0.45), halfLife 1.053 -> 1.112. Ortho bands "low", so it
// costs one cheap optional-verify property. The user's chain is the user's
// chain; this is the flag, not an edit.
